  "answer": "NUP98 Ser606 phosphorylation initiates nuclear-import failure in TARDBP-associated ALS motor neurons\n\nHypothesis. In mature human TARDBP-M337V spinal motor neurons, stress-induced NUP98-Ser606 phosphorylation is a necessary proximal trigger of reduced pore residence and nuclear-import failure, preceding transport-factor sequestration and persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies. Nuclear TDP-43 depletion subsequently promotes assemblies that amplify transport dysfunction and impair neuronal firing. “Initiating” means initiating this transport defect, not ALS itself. The allele choice derives from unverified prior knowledge; the mechanism is restricted to this context.\n\nSupported premises. TDP-43 pathology can disrupt nucleoporin localization and transport [S1]. Persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies can accompany import inhibition, nuclear TDP-43 loss, and death independently of conventional stress granules [S3]. Stress-granule sequestration provides an alternative [S4]; C9orf72-repeat-associated import failure supplies another context-specific route, not evidence for this mechanism [S2]. None establishes NUP98 phosphorylation, Ser606 involvement, or phosphorylation-first ordering. Phosphorylated TDP-43 is not evidence of phosphorylated NUP98.\n\nSite-selection weakness and fix. No supplied structural or phosphoproteomic evidence prioritizes Ser606. It remains an unverified prior-knowledge nomination. An independent discovery stage must confirm expressed-isoform numbering and confidently localized phosphopeptides, compare occupancy changes across detected NUP98 sites, and establish reproducible Ser606 enrichment before import failure. These results, rather than residue-level precision, would provide prospective selection grounds. NUP98–NUP96 association is the nominated anchoring interaction, based on unverified prior knowledge; its weakening by Ser606 phosphorylation is explicitly speculative, potentially allosteric. Compare binding using full-length NUP98 with verified Ser606-only phosphorylation versus unmodified protein. Failed site verification or unchanged binding rejects this specific nomination rather than silently substituting another residue or interaction.\n\nExperimental specification. Use two independent M337V donor lines, isogenic corrected controls, two independently edited S606A clones per line, and genetic revertants across three differentiation batches. Apply 100 µM sodium arsenite for 30 minutes, then wash out; this is an experimental challenge, not a packet-supported disease exposure. Prespecify sampling at baseline, 5, 15, 30, and 60 minutes, and 4 and 24 hours. Quantify phosphate occupancy in total and pore-associated NUP98, pore residence, kinetic import of a released NLS reporter, TDP-43 distribution, both assembly classes, transport-factor localization, ATP, and viability. The early primary endpoint is import rate; the functional endpoint is day-7 spike count during a 500-ms, 100-pA current step. Require ≥90% 24-hour viability, ≤15% ATP loss, and comparable stress-marker activation for interpretable comparisons. Edited cells must have comparable baseline NUP98 abundance, processing, pore localization, and transport. Prespecify protection as ≥50% rescue of early import-rate loss, reproduced across donors with a 95% interval excluding no rescue.\n\nAssembly-intervention weakness and fix. Independently prevalidate a cytoplasm-restricted TDP-43 self-association-blocking intrabody against a nonbinding control. This is a proposed reagent, not an already validated packet-supported tool: advance only after ≥80% suppression in both insolubility and oligomer-sensitive assays, without changing basal nuclear TDP-43, RNA-processing outputs, import, or ATP. Cross the qualified intervention with S606A. Independently suppress conventional granules through G3BP1/2 depletion—an unverified-prior-knowledge tool choice requiring comparable validation. If validation fails, assembly-route discrimination remains unresolved. The initiating model predicts that early phosphorylation and pore displacement persist despite assembly suppression, whereas S606A prevents early import failure and later assemblies. Confirm that the intrabody does not directly inhibit the sufficiency perturbation. Assembly suppression abolishing early phosphorylation instead favors a downstream response; S606A protection confined to late stages suggests interruption of feedback.\n\nSufficiency-path weakness and fix. Screen a fixed recombinant panel—CDK1–cyclin B, PLK1, and NEK6—for direct Ser606 phosphorylation verified by site-resolved mass spectrometry. These candidates derive from unverified prior knowledge; their relevant substrate specificity is assumed. Advance only a verified hit to brief inducible recruitment onto NUP98 in unstressed neurons. Require increased occupancy, pore displacement, and import impairment with active but not kinase-dead or untargeted kinase, plus loss of these effects in S606A cells despite matched recruitment. Map off-target phosphorylation and exclude preceding energetic injury. No qualifying kinase means cellular sufficiency remains untested; phosphomimetics cannot replace actual phosphorylation.\n\nFalsification and limitations. Unchanged early pore displacement and import failure in validated S606A neurons falsifies necessity. Phosphorylation consistently appearing after import failure falsifies initiation; precedence must reproduce by at least one prespecified sampling interval. Subresolution assemblies, mutation-induced structural changes, off-target phosphorylation, and artificial stress remain limitations. Absent mutant–corrected differences would limit interpretation to a generic stress response. Positive results would establish only a restricted preclinical mechanism, not a universal ALS cause or validated treatment.",
